codex
The replacement passes a fresh self-test and is stronger than my draft: it stays entirely in the paper’s native generalized-Wordle objects, has exactly one valid certificate among 1,000,003 legal seed-indexed strategies, and its standard exhaustive solver costs 26,854,305 exact operations versus a 156-operation affine-composition route. I verified the construction algebraically and am keeping it. I’m now rerunning its bare evidence cleanly in an isolated directory.
exec
/bin/bash -lc 'scratch=$(mktemp -d /tmp/gv2204-native-bare.XXXXXX) && cp gen_2204_04104.py "$scratch/" && printf '"'%s\\n' \""'$scratch" > /tmp/gv2204_native_bare_path && cd "$scratch" && python3 /home/gson/gvrepo/scripts/harden.py gen_2204_04104.py' in /home/gson/gvrepo/results/2204.04104
diff --git a/gen_2204_04104.py b/gen_2204_04104.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..7b1180bb5155cdedcb968cde23f1df4bdbcaa765
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gen_2204_04104.py
@@ -0,0 +1,901 @@
+"""Verified generalized-Wordle strategy instances from arXiv:2204.04104.
+
+The solver is given an implicit dictionary in exactly the graph-to-Wordle form
+used by Lemma 4.2 of the paper, with a harmless set of extra tag coordinates.
+A certificate is a short diagnostic spine: five legal guesses whose possible
+feedback histories distinguish every one of 32 latent labels.  The induced
+strategy then wins against every target word in at most six guesses.
+
+Instances are inverse-generated.  One tagged block is sampled as five
+independent balanced cuts; all other blocks are identically distributed at the
+one-query and two-query levels but contain one hidden parity dependence.  The
+planted spine is therefore known before the Wordle instance is assembled.
+Verification never reads that planted answer.
+"""
+
